<main id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <section class="page" id="start" aria-labelledby="h-start">
      <div class="text">
        <p class="eyebrow">Free and open source, MIT licence</p>
        <h1 id="h-start">Start your R package the way good ones finish</h1>
        <p class="lede">Good practices from the first commit: tests, automatic checks on GitHub (CI), GitHub files and checklists, in place before you write a line. Answer a few questions, download a zip named after your package, and carry on. No terminal needed.</p>
        <p class="cue"><span class="cue-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>Scroll to see how it works</p>
      </div>
      <div class="slot"></div>
    </section>

    <section class="page flip" id="risk" aria-labelledby="h-risk">
      <div class="text">
        <p class="eyebrow">Without good practices</p>
        <h2 id="h-risk">It works today. That is the trap.</h2>
        <p class="lede">A typical R package has the code and little around it.</p>
        <ul class="tree">
          <li class="has wide"><i>·</i><code>R/  man/  DESCRIPTION  NAMESPACE</code></li>
          <li class="no"><i>✕</i><code>tests/</code><span>Nothing proves a change is safe</span></li>
          <li class="no"><i>✕</i><code>.github/workflows/</code><span>No checks run on their own</span></li>
          <li class="no"><i>✕</i><code>.lintr</code><span>Every contributor writes a different style</span></li>
          <li class="no"><i>✕</i><code>LICENSE</code><span>Others can't safely use the code</span></li>
          <li class="no"><i>✕</i><code>NEWS.md</code><span>Nobody can tell what changed, or when</span></li>
          <li class="no"><i>✕</i><code>.Rbuildignore  .gitignore</code><span>Stray files reach the build or git</span></li>
          <li class="no"><i>✕</i><code>CONTRIBUTING  SECURITY</code><span>No agreed way to help or report a flaw</span></li>
          <li class="no"><i>✕</i><code>ISSUE_TEMPLATE/</code><span>Bug reports with no version or steps</span></li>
          <li class="no"><i>✕</i><code>pull_request_template</code><span>Changes merge with no record of why</span></li>
          <li class="no"><i>✕</i><code>GitHub labels</code><span>Nothing shows what is urgent or blocked</span></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="risk">The risk grows with time: a fix breaks something else, nobody remembers why a change was made, and the issue list can't be trusted.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="slot"></div>
    </section>

    <section class="page flip" id="kit" aria-labelledby="h-kit">
      <div class="text">
        <p class="eyebrow">With launchR</p>
        <h2 id="h-kit">The same package, with good practices in place.</h2>
        <ul class="tree">
          <li class="yes wide"><i>✓</i><code>R/  man/  DESCRIPTION  NAMESPACE  README.md</code></li>
          <li class="yes"><i>✓</i><code>tests/</code><span>testthat, with a first test written</span></li>
          <li class="yes"><i>✓</i><code>.github/workflows/</code><span>Seven checks, plus the website if you add one</span></li>
          <li class="yes"><i>✓</i><code>.lintr  air.toml</code><span>One code style, checked automatically for everyone</span></li>
          <li class="yes"><i>✓</i><code>LICENSE</code><span>MIT, or proprietary for a company package</span></li>
          <li class="yes"><i>✓</i><code>NEWS.md  cran-comments.md</code><span>A change log, and notes for CRAN</span></li>
          <li class="yes"><i>✓</i><code>.Rbuildignore  .gitignore</code><span>Only the right files ship</span></li>
          <li class="yes"><i>✓</i><code>CONTRIBUTING  SECURITY</code><span>Clear rules for helpers, and a code of conduct</span></li>
          <li class="yes"><i>✓</i><code>ISSUE_TEMPLATE/</code><span>Five forms that ask the right questions, or pick your own when you customise</span></li>
          <li class="yes"><i>✓</i><code>pull_request_template</code><span>Every change explains itself</span></li>
          <li class="yes"><i>✓</i><code>GitHub labels</code><span>Eleven, from bug to priority: high, next to GitHub's own; pick which when you customise</span></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="risk good">Plus a checklist for each stage, which opens when that stage arrives, and README badges you pick. What you get follows your plan, and you can customise any of it.</p>
        <p class="small">Working with an AI assistant? Add instructions it follows (<code>AGENTS.md</code>, a <code>CLAUDE.md</code> that loads it, and rules in <code>.agents/rules/</code>, under Extras when you customise), and point it to launchR's guide for agents at <a href="/SKILL.md">/SKILL.md</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="slot"></div>
    </section>

    <section class="page flip" id="checks" aria-labelledby="h-checks">
      <div class="text">
        <p class="eyebrow">Quality checks</p>
        <h2 id="h-checks">Seven checks that run by themselves.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Checks GitHub runs by itself on every change, known as CI (continuous integration). Each is a short file in your package: change it or delete it. Each pull request gets one comment that says which checks failed and what to do about each.</p>
        <ol class="checks" id="checkList">
          <li><svg class="tick" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-tick" /></svg><code>r-cmd-check</code><span>R CMD check, the core check: what CRAN runs on every package</span></li>
          <li><svg class="tick" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-tick" /></svg><code>lint</code><span>Style, docs in sync, spelling</span></li>
          <li><svg class="tick" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-tick" /></svg><code>secret-scan</code><span>Passwords and tokens committed by mistake</span></li>
          <li><svg class="tick" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-tick" /></svg><code>coverage</code><span>How much code the tests run</span></li>
          <li><svg class="tick" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-tick" /></svg><code>no-suggests</code><span>Works without optional packages</span></li>
          <li><svg class="tick" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-tick" /></svg><code>full-tests</code><span>Every test, including the ones CRAN skips</span></li>
          <li><svg class="tick" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-tick" /></svg><code>r-cmd-check-extended</code><span>Weekly R CMD check on more systems</span></li>
        </ol>
        <p class="small">Every launchR release is tested on GitHub's Linux, Windows and Mac runners before it ships, and checked against the setups of the release before it, so an update doesn't break your package.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="slot"></div>
    </section>

    <section class="page flip" id="plan" aria-labelledby="h-plan">
      <div class="text">
        <p class="eyebrow">Your plan</p>
        <h2 id="h-plan">Tell it your plan. It sets up the rest.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Where is the package headed? Each plan sets sensible defaults; choose Customise a plan to change any of them.</p>
        <ul class="plans-list">
          <li><i class="chip c1"></i><div><b>Public, going to CRAN</b></div></li>
          <li><i class="chip c2"></i><div><b>Public, not for CRAN</b><span>Installed from GitHub</span></div></li>
          <li><i class="chip c3"></i><div><b>Private for now</b><span>Public and CRAN later</span></div></li>
          <li><i class="chip c4"></i><div><b>Private for good</b><span>A QC checklist for teams with a quality process</span></div></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="small">Checklists open when that stage arrives: Before public, After public, Before CRAN, After acceptance, Next release, and QC before release for a private package.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="slot"></div>
    </section>

    <section class="page flip" id="yours" aria-labelledby="h-yours">
      <div class="text">
        <p class="eyebrow">Yours to keep</p>
        <h2 id="h-yours">Built in your browser. Yours to keep.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Nothing you type leaves your browser, and there is no server. What you download is an ordinary R package that never needs this site again.</p>
        <p class="privacy">If you accept cookies, Google Analytics counts visits and zips built, never what you type. <button type="button" class="reset" id="cookieChoice">Change your cookie choice</button></p>
        <dl class="notes">
          <div><dt>Already have a package?</dt><dd>Take only the automatic checks (CI files). R/, tests/ and DESCRIPTION are left alone. <button type="button" class="reset" id="existingStart">Set up an existing package</button></dd></div>
          <div><dt>Need the checks to stay frozen?</dt><dd>A snapshot copied into the repository, with no automatic fixes. <span class="tag">Coming soon</span></dd></div>
        </dl>
      </div>
      <div class="slot"></div>
    </section>

    <section class="page about" id="about" aria-labelledby="h-about">
      <div class="text">
        <div class="who">
          <img class="photo" src="/harsh-verma.jpg" width="84" height="84" alt="Harsh Verma" />
          <div><h2 id="h-about">Harsh Verma</h2><p class="role">R Leader · R packages, Shiny, AI · Pharma GxP</p></div>
        </div>
        <p>I lead R architecture and engineering in pharma: how teams design, build and validate R packages, Shiny apps and AI solutions, GxP-ready from the start. I still build them myself, and I build whatever else catches my curiosity.</p>
        <p>Good R work is more than a few R scripts. It needs a codebase built with care, an architecture that holds up, and a clear place in the network of products around it. A Shiny app or a package rarely stands alone: it trades data with other systems, pipelines and services that all have to work in sync. Getting there is the step from R coding to R engineering, which is what my <a href="https://insightsurge-dev.web.app/blog">R Engineering Playbook</a> is about.</p>
        <p class="why"><img class="why-hex" src="/logo-animated.svg" width="104" height="120" alt="" /><b>Why launchR exists.</b> Setting up an R package properly takes care, and it is the part most easily skipped. <a href="https://github.com/insightsurge/launchR">launchR</a> does that part once, the same way every time, so good practices are in place from the first commit, not discovered once the water is over your head.</p>
        <button type="button" class="end-start" id="endStart" aria-haspopup="dialog">Start your package</button>
      </div>
